{"story":"In the dawn-lit valley, two figures stood at a crossroads of numbers: the Sender and the Receiver. A bronze bell hung between them, its chime counting each choice as if it weighed gold. The Bellsmith spoke first, laying out a rule: the Sender may offer a portion of their own embers, up to five, to light the flame beyond the threshold. The ember sent would bloom into threefold fire on the other side. The Receiver, watching that threefold flame, would decide how much of the glow to return to the Sender, for the balance of light must be kept honest. If the Sender sends little, the flame withers; if the Sender sends much, the flame grows, but the Receiver learns that every spark might demand a price of honesty. Thus the valley taught a cycle: give, receive, test, trust.

The world beyond the bell held three places of choice: the Well of Risk, the Field of Return, and the Gate of Trust. Each time the Bellsmith rang, the two figures walked a path through these places, learning that the value of light multiplies when shared, yet remains fragile if guarded by fear. They learned to negotiate not with greed, but with a promise: that every ember sent would become a beacon for both, and every return would be a bridge back to shared dawn.

And so the myth of the Bell, keeper of sums, became the map of how to play: send with courage, return with wisdom, and let trust light the way."}
